<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Cleartext HTTP, decided in task A3 of the Triat sync work.

    The desktop sync speaks plain HTTP to a private address on the Wi-Fi
    (http://192.168.x.x:47823). Since Android 9 an app targeting API 28+ has
    cleartext denied by default, and OkHttp asks this policy before it opens
    the socket, so without an exception the client fails with
    "CLEARTEXT communication not permitted" before sending a byte.

    Why the exception is global and not scoped:
    - A domain-config matches host names or exact addresses, never a range,
      and the desktop is reached by whatever address the router handed out,
      so "only private addresses" cannot be written here.
    - Every other network call of the app uses an https:// URL, so allowing
      cleartext changes nothing for them; it only stops a stray http:// URL
      from being refused instead of served.

    Why this is acceptable for the sync itself:
    - The exchange payload is sealed (AES-GCM under a key derived by ECDH plus
      the pairing code, see sync/SyncCrypto.kt) and the pairing handshake is
      authenticated by that code, so a listener on the LAN sees ciphertext.
    - The few clear endpoints (/v1/hello, /v1/pair/start) carry only public
      values: the desktop's name, its identifier and a public key.

    What keeps the exception honest: DesktopSyncClient (task A4) must refuse
    any host that is not a loopback, link-local or private address, so the
    permission cannot be turned into sending sync traffic over the internet.
    Why base-config stays cleartext (audit M2, re-examined): OkHttp asks
    NetworkSecurityPolicy.isCleartextTrafficPermitted(host) before connecting
    and ConnectionSpec.CLEARTEXT does not override it, so the policy cannot be
    bypassed from the client. The paired desktop is stored as whatever address
    discovery found (a DHCP IPv4 such as 192.168.x.x), and a domain-config can
    only name exact hosts, never a range, so base=false would break the sync.
    A "local" domain-config would only cover mDNS names, not IPs. The http
    deep links (manifest) are rewritten to https in code instead
    (YouTubeLinkParser.forceHttps) and no external API URL is http.

    Trust anchors are spelled out (system CAs only, which is also the default
    at this target) so the file states the whole policy.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
